64,3,1,talk,Servas,,"Looking at our candidates, we must be cautious. Project B relies on greenhouses, and Project D on live colonies; however, we must ask if the specialized hands to manage them are still with us.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,1,talk,Midori,,"Project A seems very promising. It focuses on cataloging, which Toshio mentioned is more enduring, and it avoids the high animal-unit costs and greenhouse congestion that would burden the researchers of Projects B and D.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,1,talk,Kenji,,I agree with Midori! Project B is out because the glasshouses are totally full. And Project C is risky because the spectroscopy suite is unreliable and the engineers are spread too thin. Project A looks much safer!,"{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"Project A is indeed the most resilient choice. Projects B and D are far too susceptible to the whims of nature and biological volatility, while Project C risks being stalled by mechanical failure.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"Project A aligns with the historical evidence. It is a catalogue-style project, which provides enduring utility, and it avoids the breeding colonies and greenhouse dependencies that frequently lead to schedule overruns.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Servas,,"It is heartening to see such consensus. Project A avoids the void left by the departed phenotyping technicians and the absence of analysts, relying instead on a structure that is both sustainable and achievable.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,2,talk,Midori,,"I feel very comfortable with Project A. Since the national computing agreement covers simulation and modelling at no charge, the data processing for such a large atlas should be sustainable and fair for the researchers.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,2,talk,Kenji,,Exactly! Project A doesn't need the broken machines or the full greenhouses. It's the only one that actually has a clear path to getting done without getting stuck in a long line!,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"It is a rare pleasure when the path of least resistance also happens to be the path of greatest utility. Project A avoids the biological gambling of the others, ensuring the science remains the focus.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,2,talk,Toshio,,Project A is the only candidate that satisfies our criteria for stability and long-term value. It avoids the pitfalls of mechanism-focused failures and the volatility of live colonies. I vote for Project A.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Servas,,"When the spirit of the work is matched by the availability of the hands to perform it, success is inevitable. Project A is the most harmonious choice. I vote for Project A.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,3,talk,Midori,,"I agree completely. Project A is the most sustainable option, ensuring the researchers aren't overwhelmed by rising costs or facility shortages. It is the most supportive choice we can make. I vote for Project A.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,3,talk,Kenji,,I'm all in! Project A is the only one that won't be held back by broken equipment or full rooms. It's the smartest way to make sure the research actually happens! I vote for Project A.,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,Logic dictates that we avoid the volatility of field sites and the fragility of breeding colonies. Project A offers the most stable environment for success. I vote for Project A.,"{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
